      "disposition": "uncertain",
      "r8_intrinsic": "Angular, predominantly flat gravel/chip shapes remain explicit at512. At128 the image retains distinct granular cluster structure beyond color; individual coarse flecks are small but still legible. It is substantially more structurally specific than smooth pan.",
      "comparison_r7": "At512 and128 this is a modest visual change: almost the same clustered arrangement remains evident. At64/32 the broad density contrast is more subdued than r7, so the correction does improve distance quietness. The spatial topology and repeated diagonal cluster placement remain.",
      "comparison_retained_r4": "The retained r4 has softer, smaller aggregate with pronounced broad sweeping cluster bands. r8 retains a more angular, coarser material cue at128 and less painted micro-relief. This is useful progress over r4; r4 is not an unproblematic fallback because its repeating diagonal bands remain obvious.",
      "repeat_and_false_read": "r8 repeat128 still has a traceable diagonal sequence of dense chip clusters and open windows. This is not a hard seam or a continuous ridge, but the large-scale placement is a conspicuous repeat cue. Angular islands separated by pale sand can resemble fragmented crust/cells at512; no convincing dark branching crack network is present. At128 granular gravel is a reasonable reading, though not exclusive.",
      "distance": "At64 the clustered grain survives weakly; at32 palette and average tone dominate. That loss of micro-identity is acceptable as a distance limitation and is not the reason for uncertainty.",
      "reason": "Local gravel vocabulary is improved relative to r4, but the intended macro-pattern correction is not convincingly complete at128. Retain neither a confident success claim nor an automatic rejection based on32; judge the residual repeated cluster cadence in actual terrain context before choosing it."
    },
    {
      "id": "cantonment",
      "neutral_label": "C",
      "disposition": "reject",
      "r8_intrinsic": "A rougher compacted-earth-compatible surface than pan, with broad patches and small grit. At128 the strongest identifying cue is a set of oblique, paired light/dark boundaries that read like shallow ridges or undulations.",
      "comparison_r7": "r8 is visibly less contrasted and quieter, especially at64/32, but the same wave-like edge arrangement persists. The correction reduces strength without removing the objectionable structure.",
      "comparison_retained_r4": "r4 has broad smoother areas separated by irregular gritty swaths and feels more flat/compacted at128, although its repeated arrangement is still detectable. r8 replaces some useful grit-versus-smooth distinction with more conspicuous ridge-like edges. It is not a clear improvement on r4 for the stated compacted-flat target.",
      "compaction_vs_pan": "The two are distinguishable beyond lightness at128: cantonment is coarser and more edge-defined while pan is diffuse. However, this difference does not establish compaction specifically; shallow terrain relief is a strong competing interpretation. Making the surfaces distinguishable via the wrong cue does not resolve the brief.",
      "repeat_and_false_read": "Repeated oblique ridge strokes remain visible at128,64 and32. No branching cracks. False relief is the central objection, not geology disappearing at distance.",
      "distance": "Palette would normally carry much of the far-scale role; here a small repeated ridge cadence also survives. I do not require gritty micro-identity at32.",
      "reason": "Reject r8 as the claimed correction for cantonment because the broad ridge/diagonal problem remains and is stronger than in the retained r4 comparison. This does not formally approve r4 or prescribe another production iteration."
    },
